{
 "what": "Repeated full-path load runs on fresh databases to choose scorer defaults, the diagnosis of the 500/s ceiling, and the same runs after the fix",
 "date": "2026-10-22",
 "follows": [
  "bench/full_path_load_2026-10-22.json",
  "bench/full_path_followup_2026-10-22.json"
 ],
 "hardware": "Apple M3 Pro, 18 GiB memory, macOS 15.3.1; Docker Desktop with 11 CPUs and 11.67 GiB for the VM",
 "k6": "v2.3.0 (official macos-arm64 release, checksum verified), run natively against 127.0.0.1:8086",
 "workload": "bench/load_full.js, unchanged",
 "method": {
  "fresh_database": "Every measured run used its own Compose project (payguard_bench) brought up from new, empty volumes and torn down with its volumes afterwards. The project's everyday volumes were not touched.",
  "per_run_sequence": "Start the stack with the setting under test; for the no-index setting drop outbox_pending_idx and postings_journal_idx after Flyway has run; record the index count and scorer process count; run a 15 s warm-up at 100/s; wait 10 s; run the measured 15 s at the offered rate.",
  "repeats": "Three repeats of every setting and rate. The order of settings was rotated between repeats so slow drift on the host does not favour one setting.",
  "host": "Other applications were light during these runs (the busiest non-Docker process used about 40% of one core). The recorded load average includes each run's own card setup, which is why it reads 5-17.",
  "images": "payguard-api, payguard-score and payguard-console built from commit c11b991 for the first batch; payguard-score rebuilt with fraud/src/payguard_fraud/nodelay.py and the --http flag for the second batch."
 },
 "diagnosis": {
  "summary": "Before the fix, 500/s saturated with 4, 6 or 8 single-thread scorer workers alike (about 430 completed per second), although the scorer rarely missed its 50 ms deadline (at most 71 fallbacks in a run of about 5,900 authorizations). Sampling pg_stat_activity during a 4x1 500/s run showed all 20 of the API's pooled connections checked out, about 19 of them idle in transaction: the API was holding each connection while it waited on the scorer. With the model off the same load left the pool almost idle. Calling the scorer directly at 500/s gave p50 2 ms, so the scorer itself was not slow. A Java HttpClient probe from a container on the same network, with the API's settings, took 44-49 ms per call even with one thread on an idle stack, and the same for a Python client inside the scorer container over loopback, on GET /health as well as POST /score. A new connection per request took 1.4 ms; only keep-alive connections stalled. With --workers 1 a keep-alive call took 1.3 ms, with --workers 2 it took 48 ms.",
  "cause": "With --workers above 1, uvicorn 0.54 binds the listening socket itself with socket.socket(family=AF_INET), protocol number 0. asyncio sets TCP_NODELAY on accepted sockets only when sock.proto == IPPROTO_TCP, so every worker connection kept Nagle's algorithm on. uvicorn's h11 protocol writes response headers and body separately; on a keep-alive connection the body waited for the client's delayed ACK, about 40 ms on Linux. Twenty pooled connections at about 46 ms each cap the API near 20 / 0.046 = 435 authorizations a second, which matches what was measured. k6 on the Mac did not see the stall because Docker Desktop's port forwarder acknowledges immediately.",
  "fix": "fraud/src/payguard_fraud/nodelay.py subclasses uvicorn's H11Protocol and sets TCP_NODELAY in connection_made; compose.yaml starts the scorer with --http payguard_fraud.nodelay:NoDelayH11Protocol. After the rebuild a keep-alive call took 1.3 ms with 4 workers, and the Java probe went from 48.9 ms to 1.5 ms per call with one thread and from 444 to 1,698 calls/s with 20 threads.",
  "java_probe": {
   "before_fix_1_thread": "p50 48.9 ms, 21 calls/s",
   "before_fix_20_threads": "p50 43.8 ms, 444 calls/s, 40 timeouts",
   "before_fix_http_1_1_forced": "p50 43.1 ms with 20 threads (HTTP version was not the cause)",
   "after_fix_1_thread": "p50 1.5 ms, 622 calls/s",
   "after_fix_20_threads": "p50 10.2 ms, 1,698 calls/s, 128 timeouts"
  },
  "earlier_reading_corrected": "The 22 October follow-up said 500/s still saturated with 4x1, 'so the scorer is no longer the only limit'. The limit was this scorer-side stall, multiplied by the API holding a pooled connection while it waits. The first batch's 1-worker runs did not have the stall (single-worker uvicorn sets TCP_NODELAY); there the single process was CPU-bound.",
